District-heating dispatch models are widely simplified, yet their decision costs are seldom quantified.
This study distinguishes estimation bias, the objective-value difference between formulations, from decision regret---an L1-relative \emph{forward-valued} schedule gap, so termed here---
the forward-valued cost of each schedule when re-simulated using a common high-fidelity forward model with exponential heat losses,
computed-friction hydraulics, and consumer differential-pressure constraints. We assess both using a one-phenomenon-at-a-time
fidelity ladder from a copperplate model to station-resolved hydraulics. Supplying the copperplate with aggregate losses
enables an exact separation of loss and topology effects. Tests cover the Memmingen network and 135 synthetic networks
in a balanced factorial design. The copperplate underestimates operating costs by 15.1\% but produces schedules whose unprovisioned loss, forward-valued at the marginal unit, adds 46.1\%,
whereas loss-aware schedules provision that loss and stay hydraulically deliverable. Loss visibility, not spatial topology, explains this discrepancy:
losses account for 95.8\% of the copperplate-to-baseline gap and topology for 4.7\%. Across synthetic networks, losses explain a
 median 100.0\% of the gap. The topology effect remains within $\pm 0.6\%$ for trunk lengths of at least 5~km and never exceeds 2.4\%.
 Aggregate loss adders may conceal the deficiency in one network but do not transfer across scenarios.
 Forward-evaluating all 174 transmission stations and service laterals on the fixed schedule, using real component data, adds less
 than 1\% to its cost and finds no hydraulic violation, suggesting limited re-optimisation value at station-level hydraulics in this case. Spatial routing
 is immaterial under central generation; distributed generation remains open. Results assume fixed capacities,
a fixed heating curve, and radial networks; joint dispatch and sizing will be addressed separately.
